This shows the shape only. The scheme (`HMAC-SHA256` over `.`) is made up for the example and is not any provider's format. It was run with Node 24 and printed `valid true`, `tampered false`, `expired false`, and `false` for malformed signatures such as `"é".repeat(64)`, an empty string and non-hex text.

```js
import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from "node:crypto";

const KEY = "dummy-local-signing-key";
const sign = (ts, raw) => createHmac("sha256", KEY).update(`${ts}.${raw}`).digest("hex");

const verify = (ts, raw, sig, now, toleranceSeconds = 300) => {
  if (Math.abs(now - ts) > toleranceSeconds || !/^[0-9a-f]{64}$/.test(sig)) return false;
  const expected = Buffer.from(sign(ts, raw), "hex");
  const given = Buffer.from(sig, "hex");
  return given.length === expected.length && timingSafeEqual(given, expected);
};
```

Check the signature's format and compare **byte** lengths before `timingSafeEqual`. It throws when the buffers differ in byte length, so a string-length check is not enough: `"é".repeat(64)` has 64 characters but 128 UTF-8 bytes. Malformed input should return `false`, not crash the handler.

